{"findings":[{"blocking":true,"classId":"version-exemption-scope","classStatement":"Grandfathering an entire run lets newly written specs bypass a rule intended only for old specs.","document":"requirements/design.md","evidence":"Lines 61–64 exempt a validation whenever its run recorded validation under an earlier policy or resumed an earlier activation. The supporting decision in requirements/interview.md, lines 143–156, describes the same run-history rule, while lines 25–31 say only old specs receive warnings.","failureScenario":"A run validated under the old policy replaces or substantially rewrites its spec after the upgrade -> the new spec omits the failure model or uses unbounded criteria, yet spec.validate reports warnings and passes it.","id":"F1","severity":"high"},{"blocking":true,"classId":"required-doc-omission","classStatement":"The light-mode documentation bar must preserve blocking review of explicit brief requirements.","document":"requirements/design.md","evidence":"Lines 68–75 say documentation findings block only when following the text as written leads to a wrong or unsafe outcome, and classify suggestions to document more as low. The rule contains no exception for documentation explicitly required by the light brief.","failureScenario":"A light brief requires a runbook procedure, but the delivered documentation omits that procedure -> reviewers treat the request to add it as low, and the run can pass despite an unmet stated requirement.","id":"F2","severity":"high"}],"mandateAssessment":{"evidence":"The design covers all three #143 scope items, keeps verdict correction in reviewer instructions rather than mechanical rewriting, limits edits in #142 and #144 owned areas, and commits to a ready, unmerged PR. The agent decisions stay within the supplied mandate.","outcome":"PASS"},"outcome":"FAIL","summary":"The design is in mandate, but two rules could approve work that fails its stated requirements."}